\title[The Split Set Decides]{The Split Set Decides: Factor-Base Size, Smooth Yield, and the Direction of the Linear-Algebra Bound in the Single-Polynomial Quadratic Sieve}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 22, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study a design proposal for the single-polynomial quadratic sieve: shrink the effective factor base, of size $Q$, so that fewer relations are needed for the linear-algebra step. We show that the proposal is misdirected on three independent grounds.
(i) $Q$ is not a free parameter. A prime $r\nmid N$ divides some sieve value $x^2-N$ if and only if $\leg{N}{r}=+1$. So the primes $\le B$ the sieve can reach are exactly the admissible (ramified $\cup$ split) primes, and $Q(N,B)=\pi(B)-\#\{\text{inert } r\le B\}$.
(ii) Smooth yield is governed by $Q$, not by $\pi(B)$. For an arbitrary prime set $S$ we prove $2^{|S|}\le\Psi_S(X)\le(\lfloor\log_2X\rfloor+1)^{|S|}$ (the lower bound when $\prod S\le X$), the multiplicative step $\Psi_{S\cup\{p\}}(X)\ge\Psi_S(X)+\Psi_S(\lfloor X/p\rfloor)$, and a sieve-yield ceiling with exponent $Q(N,B)$.
(iii) In the cost model $\work(S)=(|S|+1)/\Psi_S(X)$, adding a prime $p$ does not raise the cost as soon as $(|S|+1)\Psi_S(\lfloor X/p\rfloor)\ge\Psi_S(X)$.
Empirically, the sieve positions actually examined fall monotonically in $Q$, by $2.4\times$ to $13.1\times$. At matched $u$, the smooth density correlates with $Q/\pi(B)$ at $+0.882$ and with $\log\rho(u)$ at only $+0.029$.
We prove the identity $\ks=\rank(\text{prefix}_{\ks})+1$, which settles the direction of the bound $\ks\le\rank(M)+1\le Q+1$, and show that both bounds are sharp. We characterize the integer test matrices that can detect a rank routine silently computing over $\Q$ instead of $\F_2$: exactly those of nonzero even determinant. We prove the error is one-sided. Finally, we prove that with $a$ full relations, $b$ partial relations and $D$ distinct large primes, $Q<a+(b-D)$ forces a congruence of squares in the same $\F_2^Q$ column space.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The quadratic sieve factors an odd composite $N$ by producing a congruence $X^2\equiv Y^2\pmod N$ with $X\not\equiv\pm Y$. Its single-polynomial form has two phases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Relation collection.} Scan positions $x\ge\lceil\sqrt N\rceil$ and keep those for which $f(x)=x^2-N$ is $B$-smooth.
\item \emph{Linear algebra.} Reduce exponent vectors modulo $2$ and find a nonempty subset summing to $0$ in $\F_2^Q$, where $Q$ is the number of primes that can occur.
\end{enumerate}
A dependency is guaranteed once more than $Q$ relations are held. A tempting optimization, which we call \emph{proposal P4}, is to lower $Q$, so that fewer relations are needed and the matrix is smaller. In a preceding round of experiments $Q/\pi(B)$ correlated with the number of relations needed at $+0.77$, which seemed to support it. This paper shows that P4 moves the wrong term.

Our contributions are as follows. Section~\ref{sec:split} proves split-set forcing. Section~\ref{sec:psi} gives exponential bounds on smooth counts. Section~\ref{sec:flip} gives the sign-flip criterion. Section~\ref{sec:dickman} explains why Dickman's function is blind to $Q$. Section~\ref{sec:rank} orients the rank bound. Section~\ref{sec:trap} analyses the $\F_2$-rank trap. Section~\ref{sec:lp} treats large primes. Section~\ref{sec:method} records methodology, Section~\ref{sec:alg} gives algorithms, and Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses future work. No new factoring algorithm results; the contribution is a corrected map of which parameters are real levers.

\section{Setting and notation}

Throughout, $N$ is a positive integer, $B\ge2$ is the smoothness bound, $\pi(B)$ is the number of primes $\le B$, and $\cP_B$ is the set of primes $\le B$.

\begin{definition}
A prime $r$ is \emph{admissible for $N$} if $N$ is a square modulo $r$. It is \emph{ramified} if moreover $r\mid N$, and \emph{split} if $r\nmid N$ (equivalently $\leg{N}{r}=+1$). Non-admissible primes are \emph{inert}. Put
\[
\cA(N,B)=\{r\in\cP_B: N\text{ is a square mod } r\},\qquad Q(N,B)=|\cA(N,B)|.
\]
Since every residue is a square modulo $2$, the prime $2$ is always admissible.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{sieve-reachable} primes are $\cR(N,B)=\{r\in\cP_B: r\mid x^2-N \text{ for some } x\in\Z\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a finite set $S$ of primes, $\Psi_S(X)$ is the number of $n\in[1,X]$ all of whose prime factors lie in $S$. The \emph{sieve yield} on $[a,b]$ is $\#\{x\in[a,b]: x^2-N \text{ is } B\text{-smooth}\}$. The \emph{model work} is $\work(S;X)=(|S|+1)/\Psi_S(X)$, the number of candidates per guaranteed dependency.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For vectors $v_0,v_1,\dots$ in a finite-dimensional space $V$ over a field $K$, let $\mathrm{pre}_k=(v_0,\dots,v_{k-1})$ and $\rk(k)=\dim\operatorname{span}(\mathrm{pre}_k)$. Let $\ks$ be the least $k$ such that $\mathrm{pre}_k$ is linearly dependent. It exists because any $\dim V+1$ vectors are dependent.
\end{definition}

\section{Split-set forcing}\label{sec:split}

\begin{theorem}[Sieve divisibility forces a split prime]
If $r$ is prime, $r\nmid N$ and $r\mid x^2-N$, then $\leg{N}{r}=+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We have $x^2\equiv N\not\equiv0\pmod r$, so $N$ is a nonzero square modulo $r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The factor base is the split set]
For every $N$ and $B$, $\cR(N,B)=\cA(N,B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $r\mid x^2-N$ then $N\equiv x^2\pmod r$. Conversely, if $N\equiv y^2\pmod r$, then $r\mid y^2-N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ramified $\cup$ split]
$\cA(N,B)=\{r\in\cP_B: r\mid N\}\cup\{r\in\cP_B:\leg{N}{r}=+1\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
If $v=x^2-N>0$, then $v$ is $B$-smooth if and only if all prime factors of $v$ lie in $\cA(N,B)$. Moreover $Q(N,B)+\#\{r\in\cP_B: r\text{ inert}\}=\pi(B)$.
\end{corollary}

The columns of the $\F_2$ relation matrix are therefore pinned to $\cA(N,B)$. Short of changing $B$ or the polynomial, the only way to change the column set is to discard admissible primes on purpose.

\begin{example}
Let $N=103\,764\,863$ and $x=10188$. Then $x^2-N=30\,481=11\cdot17\cdot163$ and $\leg{N}{11}=\leg{N}{17}=\leg{N}{163}=+1$. For this $N$ one has $(\pi(B),Q(N,B))=(25,13)$, $(95,49)$ and $(303,133)$ at $B=100$, $500$ and $2000$. In a real sieve run, $2\,215$ of $2\,215$ smooth values had prime support inside the split set.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[The CRT premise]
A proposal to control part of the factor base modulo $\prod_{p\le R}p$ requires $\theta(R)=\sum_{p\le R}\log p<\ln N$, hence $R=O(\ln N)$ and $\pi(R)/\pi(B)\to0$. The premise was also excluded by exhaustive enumeration at experimental sizes.
\end{remark}

\section{Smooth counts over a prime set}\label{sec:psi}

\begin{proposition}[Multiplicative step]
If $p$ is prime and $p\notin S$, then $\Psi_{S\cup\{p\}}(X)\ge\Psi_S(X)+\Psi_S(\lfloor X/p\rfloor)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The map $m\mapsto pm$ injects the $S$-smooth integers in $[1,\lfloor X/p\rfloor]$ into the $(S\cup\{p\})$-smooth integers in $[1,X]$. Its images are divisible by $p\notin S$, hence not $S$-smooth.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exponential floor]
If $\prod_{p\in S}p\le X$, then $2^{|S|}\le\Psi_S(X)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The square-free products $\prod_{p\in T}p$, for $T\subseteq S$, are distinct and at most $X$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exponential ceiling]
For $X\ge1$, $\Psi_S(X)\le(\lfloor\log_2X\rfloor+1)^{|S|}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $n=\prod_{p\in S}p^{e_p}\le X$, then $2^{e_p}\le X$, so $e_p\le\lfloor\log_2X\rfloor$. By unique factorization, $n\mapsto(e_p)$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Yield ceiling governed by $Q$]
If $a\le b$ and $a^2>N$, then
\[
\#\{x\in[a,b]: x^2-N\ \text{is } B\text{-smooth}\}\le\Psi_{\cA(N,B)}(b^2-N)\le(\lfloor\log_2(b^2-N)\rfloor+1)^{Q(N,B)}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $x\mapsto x^2-N$ is injective on $[a,b]$ with values in $[1,b^2-N]$. Smooth values are $\cA(N,B)$-supported by the corollary of Section~\ref{sec:split}. Then apply the ceiling.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For two moduli of the same size, sieved with the same $B$ and window (hence the same $u$), the ceilings differ by the factor $(\lfloor\log_2X\rfloor+1)^{Q(N_1,B)-Q(N_2,B)}$.
\end{remark}

\section{The sign-flip criterion}\label{sec:flip}

\begin{theorem}[Criterion]
Let $p\notin S$, $\Psi_S(X)>0$ and $S'=S\cup\{p\}$. Then $\work(S';X)\le\work(S;X)$ if and only if $\Psi_S(X)\le(|S|+1)(\Psi_{S'}(X)-\Psi_S(X))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Clearing the positive denominators, $\frac{|S|+2}{\Psi_{S'}}\le\frac{|S|+1}{\Psi_S}$ is equivalent to $(|S|+2)\Psi_S\le(|S|+1)\Psi_{S'}$, which rearranges to the stated inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The sign flip]
If $p$ is a prime not in $S$, $\Psi_S(X)>0$, and $(|S|+1)\Psi_S(\lfloor X/p\rfloor)\ge\Psi_S(X)$, then $\work(S\cup\{p\};X)\le\work(S;X)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Combine the criterion with the multiplicative step.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $S=\{2,3\}$, $X=100$, $p=5$: $\Psi_S(100)=20$, $\Psi_S(20)=10$ and $3\cdot10\ge20$. Indeed $\Psi_{\{2,3,5\}}(100)=34$, and $\work(\{2,3,5\})=4/34<3/20=\work(\{2,3\})$.
\end{example}

Three independent experimental designs (synthetic, CRT-based, and a real single-polynomial sieve), each with cross-validated instruments, found the paid number of sieve positions decreasing monotonically in $Q$, by $2.4\times$ to $13.1\times$. The correlation $\operatorname{corr}(Q/\pi(B),\ks)=+0.77$ is genuine, but it prices relations, not work. For $N=103\,764\,863$ and $B=400$ ($Q=41$), restricting to the $q$ smallest admissible primes and scanning until $q+1$ relations are found takes $1651$, $784$, $747$, $638$ and $599$ positions for $q=16$, $22$, $28$, $34$ and $41$.

\begin{designrule}
At fixed $B$, the paid sieve length decreases in $Q$. A proposal that shrinks the factor base to shrink the linear algebra moves the wrong term. The magnitude depends on $B$, and any quoted ratio should be labelled as a fixed-$B$ number.
\end{designrule}

\section{Dickman is blind to $Q$}\label{sec:dickman}

Let $\rho$ be the Dickman function: $\rho(u)=1$ on $[0,1]$ and $u\rho'(u)=-\rho(u-1)$ for $u>1$.

\begin{finding}
At matched $u=\log X/\log B$: $\operatorname{corr}(\log\rho(u),\text{density})=+0.029$ and $\operatorname{corr}(Q/\pi(B),\text{density})=+0.882$.
\end{finding}

Thus $\rho(u)$ describes how yield depends on $u$, but at fixed $u$ it is a constant and cannot explain the variation between moduli. That variation is governed by the split structure. The yield ceiling has exponent $Q(N,B)$, sieve values have prime support confined to $\cA(N,B)$, and each odd split prime divides sieve values with frequency $2/r$ rather than $1/r$.

\section{Which way does the rank bound point?}\label{sec:rank}

The results of this section hold over any field $K$.

\begin{proposition}
If $\mathrm{pre}_k$ is dependent, then $\rk(k)+1\le k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Prefix-rank identity]
$\ks=\rk(\ks)+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $k=\ks\ge1$. Dependence gives $\rk(k)\le k-1$. By minimality $\mathrm{pre}_{k-1}$ is independent, so $\rk(k)\ge\rk(k-1)=k-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $n\ge\ks$ and $M=\mathrm{pre}_n$, then $\ks\le\rank(M)+1\le\dim V+1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
In $K^Q$, if $v_i=e_i$ for $0\le i<Q$, then $\ks=Q+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The objection that the inequality is backwards is correct for the prefix rank, and the original chain is correct for the full collected rank. Both bounds count relations, not sieve positions, so they give no upper bound on sieving work. In the corrected experiments, $\ks\le Q+1$ held in $180/180$ instances, and $\ks/Q\approx0.78$, $0.71$, $0.60$ at $B=1024$, $2048$, $4096$. These ratios are properties of the instances.
\end{remark}

\section{The $\F_2$-rank trap}\label{sec:trap}

Two rank routines in a widely used computer-algebra library returned the rank over $\Q$ when the rank over $\F_2$ was requested.

\begin{theorem}
Let $A$ be a square integer matrix. Then $A$ has full $\F_2$-rank if and only if $\det A$ is odd, and full $\Q$-rank if and only if $\det A\ne0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Over a field, full rank is equivalent to nonzero determinant, and the determinant commutes with reduction modulo $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discriminating-test criterion]
The statements ``$A$ has full rank over $\Q$'' and ``$A$ has full rank over $\F_2$'' disagree if and only if $\det A$ is a nonzero even integer.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
The triangle incidence matrix
\[
T=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&0\\0&1&1\\1&0&1\end{pmatrix}
\]
has $\det T=2$, $\rank_\Q T=3$ and $\rank_{\F_2}T=2$.
\end{example}

\begin{lemma}[2-adic lifting]
If $v_1,\dots,v_k\in\Z^m$ are independent modulo $2$, then they are independent over $\Z$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\sum g_iv_i=0$, reducing mod $2$ shows that all $g_i$ are even. Dividing by $2$ and iterating gives $2^j\mid g_i$ for all $j$, so $g_i=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-sided error]
For every integer matrix $A$, $\rank_{\F_2}(A\bmod2)\le\rank_\Q(A)$.
\end{theorem}

A rational-rank routine can therefore only over-report the $\F_2$-rank, and hence only miss dependencies. A suite built from matrices with odd or zero determinant cannot detect it. A mutation test, which injects deliberately broken routines and requires the suite to reject them, makes validation falsifiable.

\section{The large-prime channel}\label{sec:lp}

Let $A$ be a set of $Q$ primes. Suppose we hold $a$ full relations $F_j$, which are nonzero and $A$-supported, and $b$ partial relations $P_i=s_i\ell_i$, with $s_i$ nonzero and $A$-supported and $\ell_i$ prime. Let $D=\#\{\ell_i\}$, and let $\rep(i)$ be the least index sharing the large prime $\ell_i$.

\begin{lemma}
At least $b-D$ indices satisfy $\rep(i)\neq i$, and for such $i$, $P_iP_{\rep(i)}=s_is_{\rep(i)}\ell_i^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Large-prime congruence of squares]
If $Q<a+(b-D)$, then some nonempty collection of full relations and paired products $P_iP_{\rep(i)}$ multiplies to a perfect square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The combined family has more than $Q$ members with nonzero $A$-supported cores. Their parity vectors in $\F_2^A$ are dependent, so some nonempty subfamily has square core product. The full product is that square times $\prod\ell_i^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $A=\{2,3\}$, $F_1=6$ and partials $2\cdot101$, $3\cdot101$, $6\cdot101$, we have $a+(b-D)=3>2$, and $6\cdot(3\cdot101)(2\cdot101)=(6\cdot101)^2$.
\end{example}

\begin{conjecture}[Large-prime collision threshold]
If the large primes lie in $(B,B^2]$ with weight proportional to $1/\ell$, then $\mathbb{E}[b-D]$ grows quadratically in $b$ until saturation, and the first time $a+(b-D)>Q$ occurs strictly earlier, in sieve positions, than the first time $a>Q$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Methodology and data integrity}\label{sec:method}

Each instrument fault was caught by cross-checking, never by inspection. The faults were: rank computed over the wrong field; a Tonelli--Shanks routine that passed $4\,692$ random pairs while carrying two defects; a gcd step that halved exponents per relation instead of summing them, so that the sieve reported no factor on every instance; and a log-sieve without prime-power terms (recall $0.54$, absolute work inflated by about $2.1\times$). Two 16-row sample tables in earlier reports had been transcribed rather than regenerated: $10$ of $16$ rows matched no stored record, one modulus was digit-transposed, and some ``ranks'' were the ceiling $Q+1$. All aggregates were exact. The tables were withdrawn, and only aggregates are used here.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:alg}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Admissible factor base.} For each prime $p\le B$, include $p$ if $p=2$, if $p\mid N$, or if $N^{(p-1)/2}\equiv1\pmod p$. The cost is $O(\pi(B)\log B)$ multiplications.
\item \textbf{$\Psi_S(X)$.} Enumerate exponent vectors recursively in time $O(\Psi_S(X))\le O((\log_2X+1)^{|S|})$.
\item \textbf{First dependency.} Insert parity vectors into an XOR basis and stop at the first vector that reduces to zero. The basis then has size $\ks-1$.
\item \textbf{Discriminating tests.} Keep random integer matrices with nonzero even determinant, include $T$, and require that a rational-rank mutant be rejected.
\item \textbf{Large-prime pairing.} Hash partials by large prime, pair each with the first one sharing it, and append the cores. A dependency is guaranteed once $a+(b-D)>Q$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion and future work}\label{sec:disc}

Quadratic residuosity fixes the columns. Yield is exponential in the number of columns. A column costs $+1$ and gains a multiplicative factor. Hence, at fixed $B$, the factor base should be as large as the method allows. The open problem is to evade the yield collapse at fixed $B$ without shrinking the column space. The candidate channels are large-prime variants (whose algebra is settled above; the collision statistics remain), multi-polynomial sieving, and per-instance adaptive $B$ (raising $B$ raises $Q$). All lie outside the single-polynomial regime. No new factoring method results.

\end{document}

